I'm OK with just disappearing functions that don't make sense.
(See the second quote above for why it doesn't make sense.)
I think Ben's still on the fence.    What's everyone else think?

I'm about to change the categorical distribution's behavior
to match the doc and treat outcomes as ranging from 1 to N instead
of the current implementation of 0 to (N-1).  This is also a better
fit with the multinomial.  This either fixes a bug or breaks backward
compatibility, depending on how you look at it.  :-)

If I understand Ben's (3) above, we would leave eigenvalues() and
eigenvectors() as they were and print deprecation warnings if
they're used in a model.  Ben -- will we need the .real() to pull
out the real part and if so, will that cause us problems?  I'd not
bother with checking complex parts are all zero and printing a warning.
There's not a good way to do that in the current setup without throwing
an exception.

There's nothing in the parser now for deprecated functions,
but I could create a set of signatures to check during parsing.

- Bob

