> To Ben:  the point is that mle can be ok for a model with just a few parameter but not for big hierarchical models of the sort that people are likely to put in stan.  If we had VB capabilities, that would be cool…

I still don't see that as a reason not to put various optimization-related stuff into Stan. If people are trying to maximize a likelihood when they have an incidental parameter problem or whatever, that is not Stan's fault. But there is presumably a set of well-defined problems where analytic gradients are not known and numeric gradients are too slow or too imprecise but they can be solved with auto-diff gradients. I'm sure it would be easy enough to add L-BFGS-B and other quasi-Newton or trust region options if the numeric Hessian wasn't working well for a problem.

Ben

